434	
435	Facts about the data, confirmed on real repositories:
436	
437	- **Refs layout:** each ref `refs/entire/checkpoints/<shard>/<id>` points at a commit whose tree has `metadata.json` and `<n>/metadata.json`, `<n>/prompt.txt`, `<n>/transcript.jsonl`, `<n>/full.jsonl` for session index `n`.
438	- **Branch layout:** branch `entire/checkpoints/v1` has the same content under `<first two chars of id>/<rest of id>/`.
439	- **Root `metadata.json`:** `checkpoint_id`, `branch`, `sessions[]`, `token_usage`.
440	- **Session `metadata.json`:** `session_id`, `created_at`, `agent`, `model`, `branch`, `files_touched[]`, `token_usage{input_tokens,output_tokens,cache_read_tokens,cache_creation_tokens}`, `session_metrics.turn_count`.
441	- **`transcript.jsonl`:** one JSON object per line: `{ type: 'user' | 'assistant', ts, content: [...] }`.
442	  - user blocks: `{ id, text }`; blocks with `type: 'image'` are skipped.
443	  - assistant blocks: `{ type: 'text', text }` or `{ type: 'tool_use', id, name, input, result? }` where `result` is `{ output: string, status: 'success' | string }`.
444	- **Commit link:** a commit body line `Entire-Checkpoint: <id>`.
445	
446	Parsing rules:
447	
448	- A user text block becomes a `user` entry. An assistant text block becomes an `assistant` entry.
449	- A `tool_use` block becomes a `tool_call` entry (`toolName`, `toolUseId`, `command = input.command` when it is a string, `filePath = input.file_path` when it is a string, `text = JSON.stringify(input)`, redacted, then cut to 4000 characters). When `result` exists it is followed by a `tool_result` entry with the same `toolName` and `toolUseId`, `isError = result.status !== 'success'`.
450	- Tool output is stripped of ANSI escape codes, redacted, and only then, when longer than 8000 characters, reduced to its first 3000 and last 5000 characters joined by `\n…[truncated]…\n`. Test summaries sit at the end, so the tail is kept longer. **Redact before truncating, not after**: a secret that straddles the cut leaves an unredacted fragment if truncation runs first. Bound the pre-redaction text to roughly head+8KB and tail+8KB windows (redact only those, not the untruncated whole) so this ordering doesn't turn an attacker-sized tool output in a cloned public repo into an unbounded regex scan; also treat a `-----BEGIN ... PRIVATE KEY-----` with no matching `END` inside a window as a match through the window's end, since the `private_key` rule can't find a terminator a window edge cut off.
451	- Every `text` and `command` passes through `redact`, following the redact-before-truncate rule above. Diff patches (see the `diffs` rule below) get the same treatment: a diff can reach an on-screen diff or flag scene later in the pipeline, so it is not exempt.
452	- A session keeps at most 5000 entries. A malformed line is skipped, not fatal.
453	- `startedAt` is the first entry timestamp, else `created_at`. `endedAt` is the last entry timestamp.
454	- A session is inside the window when `startedAt <= until` and `(endedAt ?? startedAt) >= since`. Keep the newest `maxSessions` (default 12).
455	- The same `session_id` appearing in several checkpoints is merged: keep the longest transcript, union the checkpoint ids and commits.
456	- `cloneOrUpdate`: a local path is used in place. A URL is cloned with `git clone --filter=blob:none` into `<dataDir>/repos/<owner>__<name>`, or fetched if present. Then `git fetch origin '+refs/entire/*:refs/entire/*'` and `git fetch origin '+refs/heads/entire/*:refs/remotes/origin/entire/*'`; a failure of either fetch is tolerated.
457	- `ingest` throws `NoCheckpointsError` when no checkpoint ref exists. When checkpoints exist but none fall inside the window it returns an artifact with `sessions: []`.
458	- `diffs` holds `getDiff` output for every linked commit under keys `${sha}:${path}`, each patch redacted (redact-before-truncate, as above) then cut to 6000 characters, at most 40 files per commit.
459	- Commit messages and tool output come from a cloned public repository and are not trusted input. Map the `Entire-Checkpoint:` trailer with a NUL-delimited `git log` format (a commit sha or message cannot contain NUL, unlike arbitrary control characters a crafted commit message could use to desynchronize a custom delimiter) and validate each parsed sha against a hex pattern before it is used as a git revision argument anywhere (e.g. in `getDiff`).
